# Coding Principles — Simplicity First

The #1 rule: **write the simplest code that fully solves the problem.** Always prefer the easy, obvious solution over the clever, complex one. If two approaches work, ship the one a junior dev can read in 10 seconds.

## KISS — Keep It Simple
- Solve the actual problem, nothing more. No speculative abstractions.
- Prefer plain functions and early returns over deep nesting and clever one-liners.
- A longer-but-clearer block beats a short-but-cryptic one.

```ts
// ❌ Too clever
const r = items.reduce((a, i) => ({ ...a, [i.id]: i }), {});

// ✅ Obvious
const byId: Record<string, Item> = {};
for (const item of items) byId[item.id] = item;
```

## DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself (within reason)
- If the same logic appears 3+ times, extract it into a small named helper.
- Do NOT over-DRY: a little duplication is better than the wrong abstraction.

## YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It
- Don't add config, options, or layers "for the future." Build for today's requirement.
- Delete dead code instead of commenting it out.

## SOLID (lightweight)
- **S**ingle responsibility: one function/component does one thing. If it needs "and" to describe it, split it.
- **O**pen/closed: extend via new small pieces, avoid rewriting working code.
- **D**ependency direction: depend on small interfaces/props, not on big concrete blobs.

## Naming & readability
- Names say intent: `getActiveUsers()` not `gau()`. Booleans read as questions: `isOpen`, `hasError`.
- Keep functions short. If it scrolls off screen, split it.
- Only comment the *why* (intent, trade-offs, constraints) — never narrate the *what*.

## When in doubt
Choose the option with fewer moving parts. Optimize for the next person reading the code, not for showing off.
